---
/**
 * Written for a reader, not for a reviewer of the build.
 *
 * The previous version of this page was a development history: what went wrong
 * during the rebuild, which sources turned out to be mislabelled, why a
 * per-āyah fetch mattered. All of that is true and worth recording, but it is
 * not what someone opening "About" wants. They want to know what the site is,
 * whether they can trust the text, and how to tell us it is wrong.
 *
 * The engineering reasoning lives where it belongs — CONTRIBUTING.md, DESIGN.md
 * and INVENTORY.md in the repository, linked at the foot of this page.
 */
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { DUAS, PROPHETS } from "../lib/content";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");
const prophetCount = PROPHETS.filter((p) => p.section === "prophet").length;
const hadithCount = DUAS.filter((d) => d.source.type === "hadith").length;
---

<Base
  title="About — The Duʿāʾ of the Prophets"
  description="What this site is, where its text comes from, and how to report an error."
>
  <h1 style="font-size: var(--t-xl); margin-top: var(--s-12)">About</h1>

  <div class="stack prose" style="margin-top: var(--s-8)">
    <section>
      <p class="lede">
        A reference for the supplications the Prophets made — {DUAS.length} of
        them, from {prophetCount} prophets, with the Arabic, how to say it, what
        it means, and the occasion it was said on.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="section-label">Finding something</h2>
      <p>
        There are two ways in. <a href={`${base}/themes/`}>By need</a> — sixteen
        situations, if you are looking for words for something in particular. Or
        <a href={`${base}/prophets/`}>by prophet</a>, in the order they came.
        Each prophet's page carries all of his supplications in full, so you can
        read straight through without opening anything.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href={`${base}/search/`}>Search</a> works in Arabic without
        diacritics, in transliteration without the dots and macrons, and in
        English or Turkish.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Settings</strong> — in the top corner — controls the interface
        language, which translations appear, text size, light or dark, and
        whether transliteration shows. Your choices are remembered on your own
        device and nowhere else. There are no accounts and nothing is tracked.
      </p>
      <p>After your first visit the whole site works without a connection.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="section-label">Where the text comes from</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>The Arabic</strong> is fetched from a published edition of the
        muṣḥaf and checked against a checksum every time the site is built. It is
        never typed by hand — not in an entry, not in a heading, not anywhere. If
        a single character changed, the site would fail to build.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Translations</strong> are Marmaduke Pickthall's in English and
        Elmalılı Hamdi Yazır's in Turkish. Both are out of copyright, and both
        are taken āyah by āyah, so the translation always covers exactly the same
        words as the Arabic beside it. Every translation on the site names its
        translator.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Commentary</strong> is Tafsīr al-Jalālayn, by al-Maḥallī
        (d. 1459) and al-Suyūṭī (d. 1505), in Feras Hamza's translation for the
        Royal Aal al-Bayt Institute, published at altafsir.com.
      </p>
      <p>
        Where a supplication is <strong>an excerpt</strong> from a longer āyah,
        the whole āyah is shown with the supplication in full ink and the
        surrounding narrative dimmed, and the page says so. You can always tell a
        complete verse from part of one.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="section-label">Two things to know</h2>
      <p>
        The {hadithCount} supplications from the Sunnah are the one place the
        Arabic is entered by hand, because no published ḥadīth edition can be
        checked automatically the way the Qurʾān can. Each names its collection,
        number and grading. <strong
          >Their reference numbers have not yet been checked against a printed
          edition</strong
        >, and each page says so.
      </p>
      <p>
        One entry — a psalm attributed to Dāwūd — comes from outside the Qurʾān
        and the Sunnah. It is kept separate, labelled on its own page, and is not
        presented as revelation. It is there because the Qurʾān records that he
        sought forgiveness without quoting his words.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="section-label">What you will not find here</h2>
      <p>
        No claims that reciting a particular duʿāʾ a particular number of times
        brings a particular result. Where such a promise has a sound, graded
        narration behind it, it belongs on a site like this — but none has been
        written here, and writing one without a source would be inventing
        something about scripture.
      </p>
      <p>
        Where this site offers its own thought on using a duʿāʾ, it is set apart
        and labelled an editorial note, so it is never mistaken for something
        transmitted.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="section-label">If something is wrong</h2>
      <p>
        A site that quotes scripture needs a way to be told it has made a
        mistake. If you find one — in the Arabic, a reference, a translation, a
        grading, or an explanation — please
        <a href="https://github.com/CandanUmut/dua/issues/new">tell us</a>.
        Errors in the text itself are fixed before anything else.
      </p>
      <p>You do not need to know how to fix it. Saying what looks wrong is enough.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="section-label">Colophon</h2>
      <p style="font-family: var(--font-util); font-size: var(--t-sm); color: var(--ink-soft)">
        Set in Amiri, Source Serif 4 and Source Sans 3. Open source —
        the content pipeline, the sourcing rules and the design notes are all in
        <a href="https://github.com/CandanUmut/dua">the repository</a>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
